<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Zimbabwe Rhodesia</h1><p class="st-meta">1979 CE – 1979 CE · capital Salisbury</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The short-lived state of 1979 created by the Internal Settlement, with a black prime minister but whites still holding key powers.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Under the Internal Settlement of 1978 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ian-smith">Ian Smith</a> and moderate African leaders, elections with universal suffrage were held in April 1979. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rhodesia">Rhodesia</a> was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> Rhodesia on 1 June 1979, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abel-muzorewa">Abel Muzorewa</a> as prime minister and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josiah-zion-gumede">Josiah Zion Gumede</a> as president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The constitution reserved 28 of 100 parliamentary seats for whites and left the army, police, judiciary and civil service in white hands. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patriotic-front-zimbabwe">Patriotic Front</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-mugabe">Robert Mugabe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joshua-nkomo">Joshua Nkomo</a>, excluded from the elections, kept fighting, and no country recognised the new state.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Talks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancaster-house">Lancaster House</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> produced an agreement in December 1979. The state returned to British rule under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-soames">Lord Soames</a>, and after elections won by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-mugabe">Mugabe</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanu-pf">ZANU-PF</a> the country became independent as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> on 18 April 1980.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/zimbabwe-rhodesia.jpg" alt="Zimbabwe Rhodesia" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Actarux (original) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Rhodesia_(1968%E2%80%931979).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1979 CE – 1979 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Salisbury</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abel Muzorewa, Josiah Zion Gumede</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Shona, isiNdebele</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Zimbabwe</dd></dl></aside></div>
